content.text:
The question is whether Roam's parser honours `\[` in a link label. Three cases, and the answers are visually distinct.

**Paste into any block on a scratch graph** (or a daily note you'll clean up):

```
raw:      [Paper [draft].pdf](https://example.com/x)
escaped:  [Paper \[draft\].pdf](https://example.com/x)
stripped: [Paper draft.pdf](https://example.com/x)
```

Or create them from the console with cleanup handles:

```js
const parent = roamAlphaAPI.util.dateToPageUid(new Date());
const cases = [
  ["raw",      "[Paper [draft].pdf](https://example.com/x)"],
  ["escaped",  "[Paper \\[draft\\].pdf](https://example.com/x)"],
  ["stripped", "[Paper draft.pdf](https://example.com/x)"],
];
const uids = [];
for (const [name, string] of cases) {
  const uid = roamAlphaAPI.util.generateUID();
  uids.push(uid);
  await roamAlphaAPI.createBlock({
    location: { "parent-uid": parent, order: "last" },
    block: { uid, string: `${name} — ${string}` },
  });
}
console.log("cleanup:", JSON.stringify(uids));
// for (const uid of uids) await roamAlphaAPI.deleteBlock({ block: { uid } });
```

**Reading the result — look only at the `escaped` line:**

| What you see | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|
| a link labelled `Paper [draft].pdf` | Roam honours the escape | switch to escaping, label preserved exactly |
| a link labelled `Paper \[draft\].pdf` | escape not honoured, backslashes leak | keep stripping |
| breaks the way `raw` does | escape ignored entirely | keep stripping |

The `raw` line is the control: it should show a link labelled `Paper` followed by literal text `draft.pdf](https://example.com/x)`, which is the bug the reviewer found. If `raw` renders fine, then Roam's parser is more forgiving than CommonMark and `escapeLabel` may not be needed at all — worth knowing either way.

One caveat on reading it: Roam treats `[[...]]` as a page reference, so a label containing two adjacent brackets could trigger that rather than the markdown path. `Paper [draft].pdf` has single brackets, so it exercises the case we actually care about, but if you want to be thorough, `Fig [[2]].png` is the adversarial version.

Whatever it says, it's a one-line change in `escapeLabel`:

```ts
label.replace(/[[\]]/g, "")          // stripping, today
label.replace(/([[\]])/g, "\\$1")    // escaping
```

